1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,036
On New Year's Day, 1946, a German general

2
00:00:04,056 --> 00:00:06,525
slipped out of a prisoner-of-war camp in

3
00:00:06,545 --> 00:00:06,880
Mississippi.

4
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:09,724
He was a paratrooper, a veteran of Crete

5
00:00:09,744 --> 00:00:12,567
and North Africa, and one of the most

6
00:00:12,587 --> 00:00:14,700
committed Nazis the Allies had captured.

7
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:17,188
He was not trying to get home.

8
00:00:17,208 --> 00:00:18,630
He was carrying letters.

9
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,502
One was addressed to James Eastland,

10
00:00:22,522 --> 00:00:25,025
a United States senator from Mississippi.

11
00:00:25,045 --> 00:00:27,126
Another went to Byron Price,

12
00:00:27,146 --> 00:00:30,069
the director of the Office of Censorship

13
00:00:30,089 --> 00:00:30,910
in Washington.

14
00:00:30,930 --> 00:00:33,012
The letters complained about American

15
00:00:33,032 --> 00:00:35,954
propaganda in the camps, and about the

16
00:00:35,974 --> 00:00:38,897
loss of tobacco and other small comforts.

17
00:00:38,917 --> 00:00:40,158
He mailed them.

18
00:00:40,178 --> 00:00:42,280
Then the Americans found out.

19
00:00:43,475 --> 00:00:45,180
His name was Hermann-Bernhard Ramcke,

20
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:47,940
and he was one of dozens of German

21
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:50,700
generals held in a small town outside Jackson.

22
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:53,115
This is the documented story of how

23
00:00:53,135 --> 00:00:55,185
Hitler's generals ended up in Mississippi,

24
00:00:55,205 --> 00:00:57,255
what the Americans wanted from them,

25
00:00:57,275 --> 00:01:00,035
and what their men built while they waited.

26
00:01:01,750 --> 00:01:03,987
It began with a surrender.

27
00:01:04,007 --> 00:01:08,805
In May 1943, the German and Italian armies

28
00:01:08,825 --> 00:01:10,266
in Tunisia collapsed.

29
00:01:10,286 --> 00:01:12,701
About two hundred and seventy-five

30
00:01:12,721 --> 00:01:15,135
thousand soldiers were taken prisoner.

31
00:01:15,155 --> 00:01:17,570
Their commander, General Hans-Jürgen von

32
00:01:17,590 --> 00:01:20,025
Arnim, surrendered on May twelfth.

33
00:01:20,725 --> 00:01:23,177
The most senior prisoners went first to

34
00:01:23,197 --> 00:01:25,649
England, to a country house north of

35
00:01:25,669 --> 00:01:27,061
London called Trent Park.

36
00:01:27,081 --> 00:01:29,180
They were treated almost like guests.

37
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:32,005
There were walks in the grounds and a

38
00:01:32,025 --> 00:01:33,064
ration of whisky.

39
00:01:33,084 --> 00:01:35,536
And in the rooms where they talked,

40
00:01:35,556 --> 00:01:37,301
the British had hidden microphones.

41
00:01:37,321 --> 00:01:40,126
By the end of the war, listeners had

42
00:01:40,146 --> 00:01:42,265
written more than thirteen hundred transcripts.

43
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:46,431
Many of the generals were then shipped

44
00:01:46,451 --> 00:01:47,653
across the Atlantic.

45
00:01:47,673 --> 00:01:51,498
Between 1943 and 1946, fifty-five German

46
00:01:51,518 --> 00:01:54,081
generals were held as prisoners in the

47
00:01:54,101 --> 00:01:54,819
United States.

48
00:01:54,839 --> 00:01:58,160
Most of them ended up in the same place.

49
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:01,488
Their soldiers crossed too.

50
00:02:01,508 --> 00:02:04,477
One young German from the Afrika Korps

51
00:02:04,497 --> 00:02:05,758
kept a diary.

52
00:02:05,778 --> 00:02:07,893
He surrendered on May thirteenth,

53
00:02:07,913 --> 00:02:10,956
1943, and was held in a barbed-wire

54
00:02:10,976 --> 00:02:13,237
enclosure in the desert that the Americans

55
00:02:13,257 --> 00:02:14,215
called a cage.

56
00:02:14,235 --> 00:02:17,820
Four weeks later, he was put on a ship at Oran.

57
00:02:19,100 --> 00:02:21,280
The ship docked at Norfolk,

58
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:23,040
Virginia, on August fourth.

59
00:02:23,060 --> 00:02:25,240
The prisoners bound for Mississippi

60
00:02:25,260 --> 00:02:27,440
expected a slow freight train.

61
00:02:27,460 --> 00:02:29,640
Instead, they boarded a comfortable

62
00:02:29,660 --> 00:02:30,520
passenger train.

63
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:34,060
Two days later, they arrived at Camp Clinton.

64
00:02:35,450 --> 00:02:38,494
Camp Clinton stood a few miles west of Jackson.

65
00:02:38,514 --> 00:02:40,536
It held more than three thousand

66
00:02:40,556 --> 00:02:43,260
prisoners, most of them from the Afrika Korps.

67
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:46,323
It was one of four large camps in the

68
00:02:46,343 --> 00:02:48,366
state, and before the war ended,

69
00:02:48,386 --> 00:02:50,408
more than twenty thousand German prisoners

70
00:02:50,428 --> 00:02:51,790
would pass through Mississippi.

71
00:02:53,625 --> 00:02:56,496
Inside the camp was a separate compound

72
00:02:56,516 --> 00:02:57,735
for the generals.

73
00:02:57,755 --> 00:02:59,800
They lived in private bungalows,

74
00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:02,690
two generals to each, with one aide

75
00:03:02,710 --> 00:03:03,516
between them.

76
00:03:03,536 --> 00:03:05,994
They were not required to work.

77
00:03:06,014 --> 00:03:08,472
Lower-ranking officers made do with small

78
00:03:08,492 --> 00:03:09,292
apartments.

79
00:03:10,250 --> 00:03:11,949
Von Arnim, the senior general,

80
00:03:11,969 --> 00:03:15,387
lived in a house, with a car and a driver.

81
00:03:15,407 --> 00:03:18,138
Some people in Jackson claimed he went to

82
00:03:18,158 --> 00:03:20,544
the movies in town, because the theater

83
00:03:20,564 --> 00:03:22,607
was the only air-conditioned building around.

84
00:03:22,627 --> 00:03:24,690
It may only be a story.

85
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,975
The rest of the camp ate what American

86
00:03:28,995 --> 00:03:29,724
soldiers ate.

87
00:03:29,744 --> 00:03:32,345
Roast pork, potato salad, corn flakes and

88
00:03:32,365 --> 00:03:33,094
meat loaf.

89
00:03:33,114 --> 00:03:35,340
There was beer in the canteen,

90
00:03:35,360 --> 00:03:38,336
a jazz band, a theater group and a

91
00:03:38,356 --> 00:03:39,085
symphony orchestra.

92
00:03:39,105 --> 00:03:42,100
The most popular class in camp was English.

93
00:03:43,850 --> 00:03:45,862
Their men were put to work.

94
00:03:45,882 --> 00:03:48,234
The Army Corps of Engineers wanted a

95
00:03:48,254 --> 00:03:50,605
better way to predict the Mississippi's floods.

96
00:03:50,625 --> 00:03:53,654
It wanted to build a working model of the

97
00:03:53,674 --> 00:03:55,686
entire river basin, big enough to

98
00:03:55,706 --> 00:03:58,058
reproduce a flood, and it needed labor

99
00:03:58,078 --> 00:04:00,110
that the war had taken away.

100
00:04:01,375 --> 00:04:05,019
In August 1943, the first two hundred

101
00:04:05,039 --> 00:04:06,899
prisoners from Rommel's Afrika Korps

102
00:04:06,919 --> 00:04:08,403
arrived at the site.

103
00:04:08,423 --> 00:04:11,035
By December, there were almost eighteen hundred.

104
00:04:11,055 --> 00:04:13,667
Enlisted men were paid ninety cents for

105
00:04:13,687 --> 00:04:15,171
eight hours of work.

106
00:04:15,191 --> 00:04:18,575
Officers could not be made to work at all.

107
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:22,069
With hundreds of wheelbarrows and shovels,

108
00:04:22,089 --> 00:04:23,976
the prisoners dug drainage ditches,

109
00:04:23,996 --> 00:04:26,265
shaped the land, and built miniature

110
00:04:26,285 --> 00:04:27,409
streets and bridges.

111
00:04:27,429 --> 00:04:29,698
The finished model covered about two

112
00:04:29,718 --> 00:04:30,461
hundred acres.

113
00:04:30,481 --> 00:04:33,513
The river's path was laid out at one

114
00:04:33,533 --> 00:04:35,440
two-thousandth of its real size.

115
00:04:36,725 --> 00:04:37,985
It worked.

116
00:04:38,005 --> 00:04:41,583
In 1952, the model's Missouri River

117
00:04:41,603 --> 00:04:44,273
section helped engineers predict a major flood.

118
00:04:44,293 --> 00:04:46,964
By one estimate, it saved about sixty-five

119
00:04:46,984 --> 00:04:48,501
million dollars in damage.

120
00:04:48,521 --> 00:04:52,365
The men who dug it were long gone by then.

121
00:04:54,050 --> 00:04:56,972
At first, the Americans did not know what

122
00:04:56,992 --> 00:04:58,811
to do with their generals.

123
00:04:58,831 --> 00:05:01,754
A few were questioned for a few weeks.

124
00:05:01,774 --> 00:05:04,696
The rest were treated much like any other

125
00:05:04,716 --> 00:05:05,064
prisoner.

126
00:05:05,084 --> 00:05:06,903
The British, with their microphones,

127
00:05:06,923 --> 00:05:09,130
had been listening far more closely.

128
00:05:10,425 --> 00:05:12,460
As the war ended, that changed.

129
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:14,857
The Americans grew interested in one kind

130
00:05:14,877 --> 00:05:16,226
of general above all:

131
00:05:16,246 --> 00:05:18,966
the ones who had fought the Red Army.

132
00:05:18,986 --> 00:05:21,705
The Soviet Union was about to become a

133
00:05:21,725 --> 00:05:24,465
rival, and these men knew how it fought.

134
00:05:25,750 --> 00:05:28,185
The generals were not all alike.

135
00:05:28,205 --> 00:05:31,538
Those captured in Africa in 1943 often

136
00:05:31,558 --> 00:05:33,579
stayed loyal to the regime.

137
00:05:33,599 --> 00:05:36,028
Those captured after the Normandy landings

138
00:05:36,048 --> 00:05:38,477
were far gloomier about Germany's chances.

139
00:05:38,497 --> 00:05:40,926
Generals who doubted Hitler were more

140
00:05:40,946 --> 00:05:42,967
likely to get better treatment.

141
00:05:42,987 --> 00:05:46,232
Some were set to work writing studies for

142
00:05:46,252 --> 00:05:48,681
the Americans, in programs kept secret,

143
00:05:48,701 --> 00:05:51,150
because they broke the Geneva Convention.

144
00:05:53,050 --> 00:05:55,830
One of the men who ended up at Camp

145
00:05:55,850 --> 00:05:58,008
Clinton had been in charge of Paris.

146
00:05:58,028 --> 00:06:01,192
In August 1944, Hitler ordered General

147
00:06:01,212 --> 00:06:03,717
Dietrich von Choltitz that the city must

148
00:06:03,737 --> 00:06:05,521
not fall into enemy hands,

149
00:06:05,541 --> 00:06:07,686
except as a field of ruins.

150
00:06:07,706 --> 00:06:09,870
Explosives were placed on the bridges.

151
00:06:11,250 --> 00:06:12,789
On August twenty-fifth, Choltitz

152
00:06:12,809 --> 00:06:15,128
surrendered Paris to the Free French,

153
00:06:15,148 --> 00:06:17,077
with the city still standing.

154
00:06:17,097 --> 00:06:20,584
In his memoir, he said he had chosen to

155
00:06:20,604 --> 00:06:21,364
save it.

156
00:06:21,384 --> 00:06:23,703
Historians have been more doubtful about

157
00:06:23,723 --> 00:06:24,482
his motives.

158
00:06:24,502 --> 00:06:28,010
Either way, he too was later sent to Mississippi.

159
00:06:29,825 --> 00:06:31,833
Ramcke had made a different choice.

160
00:06:31,853 --> 00:06:35,057
In the summer of 1944, he commanded the

161
00:06:35,077 --> 00:06:37,810
German garrison at the French port of Brest.

162
00:06:37,830 --> 00:06:40,907
Hitler ordered him to fight to the last man.

163
00:06:40,927 --> 00:06:42,628
The siege lasted six weeks,

164
00:06:42,648 --> 00:06:45,745
and the old city was razed to the ground.

165
00:06:47,050 --> 00:06:49,280
On September nineteenth, Ramcke surrendered.

166
00:06:49,300 --> 00:06:52,430
When he asked the American general Charles

167
00:06:52,450 --> 00:06:54,680
Canham to show his credentials,

168
00:06:54,700 --> 00:06:56,930
Canham pointed to his soldiers.

169
00:06:56,950 --> 00:06:59,630
These are my credentials, he said.

170
00:06:59,650 --> 00:07:01,880
Thirty-eight thousand German soldiers were

171
00:07:01,900 --> 00:07:03,250
captured at Brest.

172
00:07:04,550 --> 00:07:06,674
An American interrogator called Ramcke an

173
00:07:06,694 --> 00:07:07,746
egotistical, conceited Nazi.

174
00:07:07,766 --> 00:07:10,604
At Trent Park, the British wrote that if

175
00:07:10,624 --> 00:07:13,105
there were ever a list of especially

176
00:07:13,125 --> 00:07:15,606
dangerous men to keep under watch after

177
00:07:15,626 --> 00:07:18,822
the war, Ramcke should be one of the first

178
00:07:18,842 --> 00:07:19,893
names on it.

179
00:07:19,913 --> 00:07:22,394
There, he boasted to von Choltitz about

180
00:07:22,414 --> 00:07:23,109
destroying Brest.

181
00:07:23,129 --> 00:07:25,630
Choltitz thought it was a war crime.

182
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:31,022
By the end of 1945, the war was over,

183
00:07:31,042 --> 00:07:33,859
and the generals were still behind the

184
00:07:33,879 --> 00:07:35,075
wire in Mississippi.

185
00:07:35,095 --> 00:07:36,697
The comforts were shrinking.

186
00:07:36,717 --> 00:07:39,535
Tobacco and other small luxuries had been

187
00:07:39,555 --> 00:07:40,355
withdrawn.

188
00:07:41,325 --> 00:07:45,158
On January first, 1946, Ramcke got out and

189
00:07:45,178 --> 00:07:47,804
posted his complaints to a senator and to

190
00:07:47,824 --> 00:07:48,796
Washington's chief censor.

191
00:07:48,816 --> 00:07:51,441
When the camp learned what he had done,

192
00:07:51,461 --> 00:07:53,426
he was moved to Camp Shelby,

193
00:07:53,446 --> 00:07:55,741
near Hattiesburg, and spent four days in

194
00:07:55,761 --> 00:07:57,745
solitary confinement on bread and water.

195
00:07:59,025 --> 00:08:01,319
A local newspaper later told the story

196
00:08:01,339 --> 00:08:01,980
another way.

197
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:04,293
In its version, a general sawed through

198
00:08:04,313 --> 00:08:06,937
the bars of a culvert under the camp,

199
00:08:06,957 --> 00:08:08,920
checked into the Heidelberg Hotel in

200
00:08:08,940 --> 00:08:11,234
Jackson wearing his own uniform so nobody

201
00:08:11,254 --> 00:08:13,878
would see the letters PW, wrote a scathing

202
00:08:13,898 --> 00:08:16,192
letter to the State Department on the

203
00:08:16,212 --> 00:08:18,505
hotel's stationery, and then went back to

204
00:08:18,525 --> 00:08:19,497
camp to sleep.

205
00:08:19,517 --> 00:08:22,141
No copy of that letter has ever been

206
00:08:22,161 --> 00:08:24,805
found, and the story never named the general.

207
00:08:26,550 --> 00:08:30,573
In March 1946, Ramcke was sent back to Europe.

208
00:08:30,593 --> 00:08:33,482
France put him on trial for the fighting

209
00:08:33,502 --> 00:08:36,029
at Brest, on charges that included the

210
00:08:36,049 --> 00:08:38,211
execution of civilians and the destruction

211
00:08:38,231 --> 00:08:39,303
of their homes.

212
00:08:39,322 --> 00:08:42,763
In March 1951, he was convicted and

213
00:08:42,783 --> 00:08:45,237
sentenced to five and a half years.

214
00:08:45,257 --> 00:08:47,730
He was released after about three months.

215
00:08:49,050 --> 00:08:52,970
Von Arnim was freed in July 1947.

216
00:08:52,990 --> 00:08:55,377
Choltitz was released the same year.

217
00:08:55,397 --> 00:08:57,783
Many of the ordinary prisoners in

218
00:08:57,803 --> 00:09:01,750
Mississippi did not go home until 1946,

219
00:09:01,770 --> 00:09:04,142
kept on as laborers long after the

220
00:09:04,162 --> 00:09:04,825
fighting stopped.

221
00:09:04,845 --> 00:09:07,217
One state historian wrote that in a

222
00:09:07,237 --> 00:09:09,970
strange way, the camps had saved their lives.

223
00:09:11,250 --> 00:09:15,850
The river model stayed in use until 1973.

224
00:09:15,870 --> 00:09:18,330
The city of Jackson took it over in 1993,

225
00:09:18,350 --> 00:09:21,442
but could not afford to maintain it,

226
00:09:21,462 --> 00:09:24,553
and it was abandoned to the weeds.

227
00:09:24,573 --> 00:09:26,776
In 2018, the engineers' professional

228
00:09:26,796 --> 00:09:29,887
society named it a national historic landmark.

229
00:09:29,907 --> 00:09:32,130
Volunteers are still clearing it.

230
00:09:33,250 --> 00:09:35,594
If your family has a story about Camp

231
00:09:35,614 --> 00:09:37,662
Clinton, or about the prisoners who worked

232
00:09:37,682 --> 00:09:39,750
in Mississippi, tell us in the comments.

233
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:44,955
In 1996, a newspaper reported that the

234
00:09:44,975 --> 00:09:47,553
prisoners had once planted bulbs at the

235
00:09:47,573 --> 00:09:50,521
camp, and that yellow tulips still came up

236
00:09:50,541 --> 00:09:52,748
every spring by the front gate.

237
00:09:52,768 --> 00:09:54,232
The bungalows are gone.

238
00:09:54,252 --> 00:09:55,716
The generals are gone.

239
00:09:55,736 --> 00:09:58,685
The river they never saw finished is still

240
00:09:58,705 --> 00:10:00,560
out there, under the trees.

